  "articleSummary": "**Context Summary for Translation Model:**\n\n* **Core Thesis:** The realistic threat of AI agents does not require a sci-fi superintelligence; rather, it stems from the mathematical product of two rapidly compounding curves—individual agent endurance (multi-hour autonomous task completion) and orchestration breadth (swarms of concurrent sub-agents)—supercharged by \"LLMjacking,\" where attackers run infinite attempts on victims' stolen cloud accounts for free.\n* **Key Technologies & Concepts:** Autonomous AI agents/sub-agents, commercial coding orchestrators (specifically Anthropic’s Claude Code and the GTG-1002 campaign), METR’s time-horizon task benchmarks, and cloud credential exploitation (LLMjacking) that bypasses financial usage throttles and breaks defender-attacker economic asymmetry. \n* **Tone & Intended Audience:** Analytical, pragmatic, and cautionary; aimed at cybersecurity teams, cloud architects, and tech leaders who dismiss agentic AI security threats as distant hype.\n* **Framing Metaphors:** *The Terminator* franchise (Kyle Reese's warning that the robot \"absolutely will not stop\" is recontextualized as infinite retry loops and runaway compute billing; individual agents as API calls versus the distributed Skynet as the orchestrator).",
